    "created_at": "2017-10-06T12:58:52Z",
    "updated_at": "2017-10-06T12:58:52Z",
    "body": "> A bogus comment might break node iterators. Not sure why we'd risk it.\r\n\r\nDoubt there would be many cases for that (and I guess we could check with a crawler).\r\n\r\nProblem is, we are trying to keep most of original content intact unless it matches specific tag name / attribute conditions, to support all the cases where some binary and other non-HTML content is served with `Content-Type: text/html` (and there is **a lot** of these).\r\n\r\nThis might be not as problematic for a browser which tries to display such content as HTML anyway, and so we can surely invent our own token type or reuse comment to cover this case, but I'd really prefer not violating the spec if it's a change that we could make directly in the tokenizer.",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
    "reactions": {
