    "created_at": "2017-10-06T14:40:36Z",
    "updated_at": "2017-10-06T14:45:42Z",
    "body": "> I strongly suspect that when you look at older HTML parsers you'll find that <> renders and </> does not.\r\n\r\nI had to download IE6 VM to check some old parser and this is what I got:\r\n![virtualbox_ie6_default_1507300137269_47257_06_10_2017_15_36_23](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/557590/31282990-2749c17e-aaac-11e7-9223-a54e09170171.png)\r\n\r\nAs you can see, both `</>` and `</ >` were treated as an element with name `\"/\"` in IE6. If changing the legacy behaviour of `</ >` worked fine for HTML (when it became a comment), and changing `</>` to be completely ignored also didn't cause any trouble, I guess it means there is no such content that would be broken if we emit comment for both? (otherwise even the initial change would be too risky)",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
