    "created_at": "2017-10-10T09:33:12Z",
    "updated_at": "2017-10-10T09:33:12Z",
    "body": "Back when the HTML parser was first specified, there was not just IE6, but also the HTML parsers of Gecko, WebKit and Presto. Each of these were *very* different in the approach for handling errors, so typically the majority or most sane way was specified, and this was revised as browsers were first aligning their existing parsers with the spec and finding out what would break, and again later when browsers rewrote their parsers from scratch to conform with the spec. (I'm not sure how they handled this particular case, but I'm guessing at least one matched the spec.)\r\n\r\nWe're in a different situation today. This case, and HTML parsing at large, is interoperable. Therefore there needs to be a much stronger case than \"it ought to be web compatible\" to change.\r\n\r\nAs for emitting a bogus end tag token that is then ignored, I think the standard should not do that. It seems like it rather makes the spec more difficult to understand. We would also need to demonstrate that it is in fact equivalent, which is not clear to me that it would be. Consider e.g. this document, is it in quirks mode or standards mode?:\r\n```\r\n</><!DOCTYPE html>\r\n```\r\n\r\nGiven your requirement to preserve input, I think you just need to invent a new type of token, or annotate an existing type of token, to handle this case. Similarly, you would need to add extra information for other cases that is lossy in the spec's algorithm, such as whitespace between attributes, the original case of element and attribute names, whether single or double (or omitted) quotes were used around attribute values, markup of bogus comments, and so on.\r\n\r\nOther HTML parsers might have different requirements, like minimizing memory usage/garbage.",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
