    "created_at": "2017-10-10T10:13:12Z",
    "updated_at": "2017-10-10T10:13:12Z",
    "body": ">  such as whitespace between attributes\r\n\r\nIt's already part of the tokens. What I meant by \"the only one\" is that if you track locations of each token, they cover entire input no matter what it is, and only this one is ignored. Which means for anything else we can preserve and restore raw input just from tokens without diverging from the spec even a bit, but for this case we would need to invent a new arbitrary token type, ignore it when running HTML5Lib tests and so on. That's certainly doable, but if there was a way to avoid messing around, I wanted to explore what it could look like on the common grounds.\r\n\r\n> Consider e.g. this document, is it in quirks mode or standards mode?\r\n\r\nYeah, didn't think about this case. It surely becomes different, but we can workaround that fairly easily.\r\n\r\n> It seems like it rather makes the spec more difficult to understand.\r\n\r\nI thought it would be the opposite (removing the only case where we don't emit anything, and reusing one of existing branches of `</` processing). ",
